{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 27, :comments ["<b>And he saw the wagons which Joseph sent, etc.</b> Rashi explained: he gave them a sign in what he was occupied with when he parted from him — in the section of the broken heifer; therefore 'and he saw the wagons which Joseph sent' and not which Pharaoh sent. And this explanation is very far-fetched, for is it Rashi's way to remove the verse from its plain sense entirely? For the verse says ״אֲשֶׁר שָׁלַח יוֹסֵף לָשֵׂאת אוֹתוֹ״ (Genesis 45:27), and furthermore, why do I need wagons — one heifer would suffice. Therefore I say that even Rashi understood 'wagons' according to its plain sense, but as for what is stated 'which Joseph sent' and it is not stated 'which Pharaoh sent,' Rashi understood from this that 'sent' means escorting, that Joseph provided escort for those wagons which were sent. For so it is stated ״וַיְשַׁלַּח אֶת אֶחָיו וַיֵּלֵכוּ״ (Genesis 45:24), and this sending is nothing but escort. And Joseph learned the law of escort from Jacob, for so it is stated (Genesis 37:13) ״לְכָה וְאֶשְׁלָחֲךָ אֲלֵיהֶם״ — and after he goes, will he send him? And what would the verse be lacking if it said 'come to them'? Rather, he said this to escort him. And Jacob learned the law of escort from Abraham, for it is stated (Genesis 18:16) ״וְאַבְרָהָם הוֹלֵךְ עִמָּם לְשַׁלְּחָם״, which Rashi explained: to escort them. And this is what is stated ״וַיִּשְׁלָחֵהוּ מֵעֵמֶק חֶבְרוֹן״ (Genesis 37:14) — for this sending is escort, and he learned the law of escort from the valley of that righteous one who is buried in Hebron, for Abraham began with escort: ״וַיִּטַּע אֶשֶׁל״ (Genesis 21:33) — notarikon: eating, drinking, escort. And when Jacob escorted Joseph, presumably he told him the commandment of escort, whose source is from the section of the broken heifer, where the elders of the city say ״יָדֵינוּ לֹא שָׁפְכוּ אֶת הַדָּם הַזֶּה״ (Deuteronomy 21:7) — that we did not let him depart without escort (Sotah 45b). And so Joseph did as well: when he escorted his brothers, presumably he took leave of them from within words of halakhah, ״הֲלִיכוֹת עוֹלָם לוֹ״ (Habakkuk 3:6), to tell them the law of escort with which he was occupied. And it is written ״וַיְדַבְּרוּ אֵלָיו אֵת כָּל דִּבְרֵי יוֹסֵף אֲשֶׁר דִּבֶּר אֲלֵהֶם״ (Genesis 45:27), and they included in this all the words — both the words which he spoke to them in his house, and the words which he spoke to them when he escorted them, and there he spoke with them about the law of the section of the broken heifer." "<b>And he saw the wagons.</b> This seeing is about contemplation, for Jacob contemplated what they told him — that Joseph put the halakhah into practice and escorted the wagons. Through this Jacob knew that he was Joseph, for in all the land of Egypt there is not a single doer of good, not even one who keeps the charge of HaShem; therefore ״וַתְּחִי רוּחַ יַעֲקֹב אֲבִיהֶם״ (Genesis 45:27). But from the words which Joseph put in their mouths there is no proof, for perhaps Joseph taught this law to others; but from the deed he drew proof. And as for the fact that he hung the escort on the wagons — and surely he did the escorting for the sake of his brothers and not for the sake of the wagons, and he should have said 'and he saw that he sent his brothers' — the explanation of the matter is thus: for it is known that what they said, 'we did not let him depart without escort,' is not a halakhah without a reason, for what does escort have to do with 'our hands did not spill this blood' (Deuteronomy 21:7)? And the reason for the matter is that anyone who departs without escort from the city, there is reason to suspect that perhaps bandits will see that he is walking alone and will think that he has no redeemer or kinsman, for no one finds his departure difficult for any person, for he has no blood-avenger, neither son nor brother; therefore whoever finds him will kill him, thinking that there is no one who will demand his blood from his hand. And even bandits who did not see him leaving the city — nevertheless, at the very least, the escort provides a small measure of protection, for it shields from bandits who see that people are escorting him and that he has redeemers; then they are afraid to attack him and kill him, lest they investigate and search after his relatives or friends, and a seeker of blood will find them and demand his blood from their hand. It is also close to say that ordinary bandits do not attack him except after inquiry and investigation whether someone escorted him or not." "And according to this reason, there is room to say that this commandment belongs to the intelligible, and if so there is no proof from the escort that Joseph gave his brothers that he is Joseph — for perhaps that ruler of the land is a righteous man and did the escorting for them from the side that it is something reason requires, for whoever does not escort is like one who spills blood; therefore it is fitting to do so for every traveler on the road. But when Jacob saw the wagons which he sent and escorted, then Jacob said 'truly the thing is known,' for this man fulfilled the escort from the side of the law of the Torah and not from the side that reason requires this matter — for in all of Egypt the wagons are not given except by order of Pharaoh, because the wagons are royal equipment, as it is written (Genesis 45:21) ״וַיִּתֵּן לָהֶם יוֹסֵף עֲגָלוֹת עַל פִּי פַרְעֹה״. It appears from this that the wagons were specially designated for the king's chariotry, and one who travels with those wagons — which are recognized and known to be from the king's house — does not need escort to save him from bandits, for without this the fear of the kingdom is upon them, both in the land of Egypt and in the land of Canaan, for everyone needed the master of the grain in years of famine. And if so, for what purpose did the ruler escort the brothers who went with the select chariots of the king — for what man would come after the king or after his chariot and camp? Rather, surely that man did the escorting from the side of the law of the Torah, for it is a decree of the verse and makes no distinction between one escort and another. Therefore, when he saw and contemplated the wagons which Joseph sent and escorted, and knew that he did so from the side of the commandment of HaShem, blessed be He, and there is none in all Egypt who keeps the charge of HaShem except the seed of Jacob — therefore ״וַתְּחִי רוּחַ יַעֲקֹב אֲבִיהֶם״." "And in my work עוֹלְלוֹת אֶפְרַיִם, discourse 173, we explained that he learned from the section of the broken heifer that Joseph was alive, for just as they measure a physical measurement to see to which city the slain is closest and to impute the guilt to that city, so too they measure a conceptual and intelligible measure — for which sin did this one die: for his own sin, for the sin of his relatives, or for the sin of the people of his city. And whoever is closest to the slain, to impute it to him — he requires atonement. Therefore Joseph sent him the section of the broken heifer, so that from within it Jacob would contemplate: which sin caused him, Jacob, to be in distress all these days? And he would search and find that this sin — that he did not fulfill the commandment of honoring father and mother for twenty-two years — caused him to be in this distress for twenty-two years, that he too would not be honored by his most beloved son. And from this he contemplated that surely Joseph was alive, for had he died, then Jacob would have been in this distress for more than twenty-two years and would have suffered beyond what he sinned, and the punishment would not have been measure for measure. Therefore, when he saw the wagons and remembered the section of the broken heifer, immediately ״וַתְּחִי רוּחַ יַעֲקֹב אֲבִיהֶם״ (Genesis 45:27)."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 45, :notes {0 "The notarikon אכילה שתיה לויה (eating, drinking, escort) expands אש\"ל from Genesis 21:33, following Rashi ad loc.; Aramaic מָרֵי דְחִטַּיָּא is the Targum's rendering for Joseph's Egyptian title.", 1 "The rabbinic dictum לא פטרנוהו בלא לויה ('we did not let him depart without escort') is from Sotah 45b, interpreting Deuteronomy 21:7.", 3 "עוֹלְלוֹת אֶפְרַיִם is the Kli Yakar's own earlier work; the page-number reference אות תקל״ג (sign 173) is rendered as 'discourse 173' following the work's enumerative format."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:34:02.005900348Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}